WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.163
What you see on screen is the solar system.

00:00:03.163 --> 00:00:04.781
The Sun at the center.

00:00:04.781 --> 00:00:06.988
Eight planets in their orbits.

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:09.636
Pluto and Ceres if you ask for them.

00:00:09.636 --> 00:00:17.800
The positions you see are the actual positions today — Keplerian elements computed for the current Julian date.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.566
Drag to rotate the view.

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:21.478
Scroll to zoom in and out.

00:00:21.478 --> 00:00:28.687
Click any planet, and the right panel opens with its physical data, orbit parameters, and gallery.

00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:30.452
Look at the orbit lines.

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:34.351
Mercury makes one trip every eighty-eight Earth days.

00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:37.661
Mars takes one point eight eight Earth years.

00:00:37.661 --> 00:00:41.191
Saturn takes twenty-nine point five Earth years.

00:00:41.191 --> 00:00:43.913
Neptune takes one hundred sixty-five.

00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:51.563
Neptune has been in its current position, relative to the Sun, exactly once since its discovery in 1846.

00:00:51.563 --> 00:00:56.344
It just completed its first full orbit, by our calendar, in 2011.

00:00:56.344 --> 00:00:58.035
Watch the planets move.

00:00:58.035 --> 00:01:05.759
The time slider at the bottom lets you speed up the simulation — one day per second, ten days, a hundred.

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:09.289
Pause it on any date and read off the positions.

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:10.540
Notice the scale.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:24.589
The orbit radii in this view are compressed on a logarithmic scale, because if we drew them to true scale you wouldn't be able to see anything past Jupiter without scrolling for half an hour.

00:01:24.589 --> 00:01:27.531
The orbital velocities are real, though.

00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:32.239
Earth moves at thirty kilometers per second relative to the Sun.

00:01:32.239 --> 00:01:35.769
You don't feel it because you're moving with it.

00:01:35.769 --> 00:01:39.079
Try the Science Lens toggle at the top right.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:48.053
It overlays additional physical data — orbital eccentricity, axial tilt, the inclination of each orbital plane to Earth's.

00:01:48.053 --> 00:01:59.013
Hover any planet with the lens on and you'll see the velocity number change as it moves along its ellipse — faster at perihelion, slower at aphelion.

00:01:59.013 --> 00:02:00.484
Vis-viva, in action.

00:02:00.484 --> 00:02:01.514
Click the Sun.

00:02:01.514 --> 00:02:02.617
Read the panel.

00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:06.736
Then click any planet, and trace its path with your eye.

00:02:06.736 --> 00:02:20.124
Every other screen in this Orrery — the mission maps, the station explorers, the fleet — was made to explain a single object that you can see right now, here, moving with the others.

